  the leaves and they spent a lot of time  digesting like cows this is kind of an  equivalent to  what ruminants do and now let's look at  the national bird of peru  this is the gallito de rolas rocas or  the  [ __ ] of the rock here we have a male  this is a bird that is still pretty  common in  most of the indian countries not only  peru  in the cloud forest and you can actually  arrange a visit to one of the lakes  these are the  places where they display you can see  that this  crest here is a kind of flattened  on the sides two layers of feathers  folding over the bill  it's only found in males females are  rubber brown  and the name gagito de las rocas  maybe refers to the fact that they spent  the nest in little caves and  attached their mud nest to a rocky wall  so then let's look at the bird  chosen by the country of uh suriname  this is in english great kiskady  but in most countries the bird gets the  name  onomatopeia related to its song it's a  type of  tyrant flycatcher and the  the song to the english speaker sounds  like kiskady  but to a person in suriname it's greek  or hate tb if you are a dutch inhabitant  of suriname  they also occur in the united states in  southernmost texas  and they go all the way to northern  argentina so it's a pretty widely  distributed  bird but very charismatic found in  cities and  can actually easily be seen throughout  south america  so now let's look at the national bird  of brazil  sabia laranjera as it's called in  portuguese and in english  rufo's bellied thrash or robin  same as the north american robin these  are  birds common throughout suburban  rural different habitats that are  altered by humans  and it's one of the most common birds in  this  lower southern cone of south america in  uruguay paraguay argentina  and parts of southeastern brazil this  bird  maybe looks not the most colorful but  has a fantastic song and this is  same with with the costa rican  equivalent  was chosen for the song and for its  commonness in human inhabited  habitats then this colorful  bird here is to a north american  bird watcher it's an oriole but this  particular one is called tropial  and in venezuela where it's a national  bird  uh they call it turpial venasulano or  guajiro  because it's found in uh original  wahira among others uh it's uh  also found in jason colombia and it's  one of the largest  species of orioles and it's a bizarre  species indicating the reason  for that is that doesn't build a typical  hanging  hammock type nest but it actually usurps  takes over nests from other species it  has to be a covered nest so it could be  another oreo or cassik or even could be  a stick nest of some  fornarid then  let's look at the bird chosen by  paraguay as a national bird  this is actually here's the right  species but i only have a  immature male so this one here  you can see that still has a lot of kind  of greenish  gray feathers but already developing its  bare throat in english  it's called bare throated bell bird  and this bird here i'm holding is a  white bell bird as related species but  you cannot just imagine that this one  would look like this pure white  and just have a brilliantly bluish green  bare throat and the name uh  in paraguay where it's a national bird  it's called  gura kampana or gurapong  pong is onomatopeia this is  the birth is most famous for its loud  song it's one of the loudest  bird vocalizations and it does sound a  bit like a  pong or like an anvil hit with a hammer  very loud and usually from the forest  canopy  then let's look at the national bird of  uruguay  te rotero or it's called southern  lapwing in english  it has a very thin little crest  has very interestingly spurs  in the wings you can maybe just barely  see a little orange  spur let me see oh here we go and these  could be used  in nest defense uh when they chase away  a fox or  and the bird is very charismatic loud uh  attacks  an intruder to his territory especially  when the chicks  or eggs are around  so in adjacent brazil it's also  onomatopoeically called quero quero so  terotero queroquero  and the species now expands and goes  pretty far north  reaching central america and it takes  advantage actually from human  disturbance and clearing of the forest  now we're switching  here's the last example and this one is  a national bird of argentina  at this brown bird is  called either ornero or alfon alonsito  or casarita and the fame for this bird  is  is their nest so here if you can maybe  zoom in at this  fortress of mud these nests are part of  the landscape  and a pair of these in a sense that they  are found on buildings on telephone  poles  it's a really a common sight in  argentina  just in brazil paraguay uruguay  the nest is made by two of these birds  by a pair  and they uh somebody calculated to use  like 3 000 bigfools of mud and  this is mud mixed with plant material so  it's like adobe  very durable and only members of this  one group of birds genus furnarios built  this  big five kilogram type fortresses

